/* book Worked example: Q: Design a two-way slab for a room 3.4 m × 4.4 m (clear) on 230 mm beams. Two adjacent edges are discontinuous and the corners are held down. Live load 3 kN/m², floor finish 1 kN/m², M20, Fe415, 20 mm cover, 8 mm bars.
   1. Depth: continuous two-way slab, Fe415: lx/D ≤ 32, so D ≥ 3496/32 = 109 mm (taking lx ≈ 3.5 m). Adopt D = 120 mm; dx = 120 − 20 − 4 = 96 mm, dy = 96 − 8 = 88 mm.
   2. Spans: lx = 3.4 + 0.096 = 3.496 m, ly = 4.4 + 0.088 = 4.488 m (both less than centre to centre). ly/lx = 1.284 ≤ 2: two-way.
   3. Loads: 3.0 + 1.0 + 3.0 = 7.0 kN/m²; wu = 10.50 kN/m², and wu lx² = 10.50 × 3.496² = 128.33 kN.
   4. Coefficients at 1.284: αx(−) = 0.060 + (0.065 − 0.060) × 0.838 = 0.0642; αx(+) = 0.0484; αy(−) = 0.047, αy(+) = 0.035.
   5. Moments (kN·m/m): Mx(−) = 8.24, Mx(+) = 6.20, My(−) = 6.03, My(+) = 4.49. The largest is far below Mu,lim = 0.138 × 20 × 1000 × 96² = 25.4 kN·m.
   6. Steel (Ast formula, then spacing ≤ 3d and 300 mm): Mx(−) 251 mm² → 8 @ 190 top (265); Mx(+) 187 → 8 @ 260 bottom (193); My(−) 199 → 8 @ 250 top (201); My(+) 146, raised to the minimum 144 → 8 @ 260 bottom (193).
   7. Edge strips (0.44 m and 0.56 m wide): minimum steel 144 mm²/m, 8 @ 300 mm.
   8. Torsion steel: the corner of the two discontinuous edges needs 0.75 × 187 = 140 mm²/m in each of four layers over 0.70 m × 0.70 m; 8 @ 300 (168 mm²/m) top and bottom both ways. The two corners with one continuous edge need half, 70 mm²/m, also 8 @ 300 (the spacing limit governs).
   9. Shear: Vu = wu lx/2 = 18.35 kN/m (safe: the short span really takes 73 % of the load). τv = 0.191 N/mm²; with pt = 0.28 % at the support, kτc = 1.30 × 0.372 = 0.48 N/mm². Safe.
   10. Deflection: lx/D = 3496/120 = 29.1 ≤ 32. Safe.
   Answer: D = 120 mm. Short span: 8 mm @ 260 bottom, 8 mm @ 190 top at the continuous edge. Long span: 8 mm @ 260 bottom, 8 mm @ 250 top at the continuous edge. Torsion mesh 8 mm @ 300 over 0.70 m at the corners. */
